menu "Camera Sensor (IMX add-on)"

    config CAMERA_IMX219
        bool "Support IMX219 (Raspberry Pi Camera Module v2)"
        default n
        help
            Enable the Sony IMX219 MIPI-CSI driver.

            Verified on hardware as of 0.2.0: streaming, auto-exposure through
            esp_video's 3A loop, stills and H.264 video.

            Still off by default only because a build should not carry two
            sensors' register tables when it uses one. Turn it on for an IMX219
            and turn CAMERA_IMX708 off.

    if CAMERA_IMX219

        config CAMERA_IMX219_AUTO_DETECT_MIPI_INTERFACE_SENSOR
            bool "Auto-detect IMX219 on the MIPI-CSI interface"
            default y
            help
                Register the IMX219 in the esp_cam_sensor auto-detect list so
                esp_video probes for it automatically at start-up.

        config CAMERA_IMX219_MIPI_IF_FORMAT_INDEX_DEFAULT
            int "Default MIPI-CSI mode index"
            default 0
            range 0 2
            help
                Which entry of the driver's mode table the sensor comes up in.

                  0 - 1640x1232 RAW10 30fps, 2x2 binned, full field of view.
                      The general-purpose default.
                  1 - 3280x2464 RAW10 15fps, full resolution. Note this is
                      wider than the ESP32-P4's ~1920 px datapath limit and
                      will produce duplicated columns; it is not usable yet.
                  2 - 1632x1232 RAW10 30fps. Mode 0 with 8 columns trimmed so
                      the width is a whole number of 16-pixel H.264
                      macroblocks. Choose this for video: the P4's ISP crop
                      only exists on chip revision v3.0 and later, and the
                      H.264 core derives its line stride from the width it is
                      given, so a non-multiple-of-16 width shears the picture.
                      Costs 0.5% of the horizontal field of view.

    endif # CAMERA_IMX219

    config CAMERA_IMX708
        bool "Support IMX708 (Raspberry Pi Camera Module 3 / NoIR 3)"
        default y
        help
            Enable the Sony IMX708 MIPI-CSI driver. The default mode is a 2x2
            binned 1920x1080 RAW10 stream. Autofocus lives in a separate driver
            (the DW9807 VCM below) because it is a separate chip; PDAF is not
            driven.

    if CAMERA_IMX708

        config CAMERA_IMX708_AUTO_DETECT_MIPI_INTERFACE_SENSOR
            bool "Auto-detect IMX708 on the MIPI-CSI interface"
            default y
            help
                Register the IMX708 in the esp_cam_sensor auto-detect list so
                esp_video probes for it automatically at start-up.

        config CAMERA_IMX708_MIPI_IF_FORMAT_INDEX_DEFAULT
            int "Default MIPI-CSI mode index"
            default 0
            range 0 4
            help
                Which entry of the driver's mode table the sensor comes up in.

                All five are the same 2x2-binned RAW10 readout at 28 fps and
                differ only in how much of it is digitally cropped away, so a
                smaller mode buys CSI bandwidth, PSRAM and encode time, and
                pays in field of view. It does not make the sensor faster -
                every mode reads out at 28 fps - but it does recover frames the
                pipeline drops at full size: imx708_video measures 27.3 fps at
                1920x1080 and 28.0 at every smaller mode. Exposure range does
                not change. Nothing is scaled: a smaller mode is a narrower window on
                the middle of the scene, not the whole scene shrunk.

                  0 - 1920x1080, 83% of the field each way. The general-purpose
                      default and the mode the examples were measured at.
                  1 - 1280x720, 56% of the field each way, 44% of the pixels.
                      Both axes are whole 16-pixel H.264 macroblocks, so unlike
                      1080 this needs no height trim before the encoder.
                  2 - 1024x768, 44% of the field across and 59% up and down,
                      38% of the pixels. 4:3, so it sees less than mode 1
                      horizontally and more vertically. The only mode whose
                      width and height are both whole 16-pixel macroblocks
                      (64x48) - even 1080 is not - so nothing has to be
                      trimmed before the encoder.
                  3 - 800x600, 35% of the field across and 46% up and down,
                      23% of the pixels. SVGA, the middle 4:3 step. 800 is 50
                      whole macroblocks but 600 is 37.5, so like 1080 - and
                      unlike every other mode here - it needs the encoder's
                      height trim.
                  4 - 640x480, 15% of the pixels. A tight window - 28% of the
                      field horizontally and 37% vertically - and 4:3 out of a
                      16:9 field, so it frames the scene differently rather
                      than showing less of it evenly.

                Modes are ordered largest to smallest.

                An application can override this at run time: pass a mode from
                imx708_format_by_index() or imx708_format_by_size() to
                esp_video's VIDIOC_S_SENSOR_FMT before allocating buffers.

    endif # CAMERA_IMX708

endmenu

menu "Camera Motor (IMX add-on)"

    rsource "motors/dw9807/Kconfig.dw9807"

endmenu
